functors we get from D(I)  to  D(e)\inserted{$=\mathbb{D}$}( the ``stripped'' triangulated catego-%
ry) can be viewed as \uline{a substitute for taking},  \uline{respectively},  \uline{direct and}
\uline{inverse limits in T} (for a system of objects indexed by I),  \uline{which in}
\uline{the usual sense dont generally exist in} \hand{$\mathbb{D}_{o}$} (except just finite sums and
products).  These operations admit as important special cases (when I
is either\inserted{one} of the two\inserted{mutually dual} categories

\margin{(**)}
