the operation of \hand{(}bi\unsure{n}ary\hand{)} amalgamated sums or fibered products,  and
hence also of taking ``cofibers'' and \xout{f} ``fibers'' of maps,  in the \xout{Sxxxxxx}
\xout{Sxxxx} sense introduced by Cartan-Serre in homotopy theory about thirty
years ago.  I also checked that the two mutually dual Dold-Puppe sequen-%
ces follow quite formally from the set-up.  One just has to fit in a
suitable extra axiom to insure the usual exactness properties for these
sequences.

Except in the commutative case when starting with an abelian cate-%
gory\inserted{as above}, I did not check however that \xout{xxx} there is indeed such ``higher
variance structure'' in the usual cases\hand{,} when a typical ``triangulated
category'' in some sense or other turns up,  for instance from a model
category in Quillen's sense.  What I did check \deleted{in}\inserted{though} this last case,  under
a mild additional assumption which seems verified in all practical cases,
is the existence of the operation  $f_! = \hand{$\int$}_I$  (``integration'' ) and
$f_* = \hand{$\prod$}_I$(cointegration) for the special case $f\colon I \hand{$\longrightarrow$} e$,  when I is either
one of the two categories\inserted{(**)} above.  I expect that working some more,  one
should get under the same assumptions \deleted{the \xout{xxxx}} at least the existence of
$f_!$ and $f_{\hand{$*$}}$  for any map $f\colon I \hand{$\longrightarrow$} J$ between\inserted{finite} ordered sets.

My main interest of course at present is in the category (Hot)
itself,  more generally in Hot($\underline{W}$) = $\underline{W}^{-1}$(Cat),  where $\underline{W}$ is a ``basic loca-%
lizer''.  More generally,  if (M,W)  is any modelizer say,  the natural
thing to do,  paraphrasing (*),  is for any indexing category I to
endow  $\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}$(I,M)  with the set of arrows $W_I$  defined by componentwise
belonging to W ,  and to define
\[ D_{(M,W)}(I) = D(I) \overset{\mathrm{def}}{=} W_I^{-1}\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}(I,M) \quad , \]
with the obvious contravariant dependance on I,  denoted by $f^*$  for
