6\unsure{\hand{7}}.    A little more pondering and scribbling finally seems to show
that the \deleted{real} key for an understanding of modelizers is'nt really the
notion of contractibility,  but rather the notion of aspheric objects
(besides,  of course,  the notion of weak equivalence).  At the same time
it appears that the notion of an \uline{aspheric} \uline{map} in (Cat),  more specifical-%
ly of a $\underline{W}$-aspheric``map''\xout{xxxx} (i.e. functor between small categories) is
a lot more important than \inserted{being just} a highly expedient technical convenience,  as
it has been so far  -  it is indeed one of the basic notions of the theo-%
ry of modelizers we got into.  As a matter of fact,  I should have known
this for a number of weaks already, \inserted{ever} since I did some scribbling about
the plausible notion of ``morphism'' between test-categories (as well as
their weak and strong variants),  and readily convinced myself that the
natural ``morphisms'' here were nothing else but the aspheric functors bet-%
ween those categories.  I kind of forgot about this,  as it did'nt seem
too urgent to start moving \inserted{around} the test category I was working with.  If I \xout{xx}
had been a little more systematic in grinding through the usual functori-%
alities,  as soon as a significant notion (such as the various test noti-%
ons)  appears,  I presumably would have hit upon the crucial point about
modelizers and so-called ``asphericity structures'' a lot sooner,  without
going through the long-winded detour of homotopy structures\hand{,} and \inserted{the still} extreme-%
ly special types of test functors suggested by contractibility assumpti-%
ons.  However \xout{it} I believe that most of the work I went through,  \xout{xxx}
although irrelevant for the ``asphericity story'' itself,  will still be
useful,  especially when it comes to pinpointing the so-called ``canonic\unsure{\hand{al}}
modelizers,  whose modelizing structure is intrinsicly determined by the
category structure.

First thing now which we've to do is to have a closer look at the
meaning of asphericity for \inserted{a} functor\xout{s} between  small categories.  There is
no reason whatever to \xout{\unsure{restrict to}} make any restrictions on these catego-%
ries besides smallness  (namely the cardinals of the sets of objects and
arrows being in the ``universe'' we are implicitly working in,  or even
more stringently still,  A and B to be in (Cat)  namely to be objects
of that universe).  Thus we will not assume A and B to be test categories
or the like.  \xout{However,} We will be led to consider,  for \uline{any} small cate-%
gory A ,  the localization of A\^{} with respect to $\underline{W}$-equivalences,  which
I'll denote by $(\mathrm{Hot}^{\hand{$W$}}_A)$ \hand{or simply $(\mathrm{Hot}_A)$:}
\[ \hand{(1)} \qquad \hand{$(\mathrm{Hot}^{W}_A)$ or} \ (\mathrm{Hot}^{\deleted{\illegible{1}}}_A) \ = \ \underline{W}_A^{-1}A\hat{} \quad . \]

