\over{v}{c}ategories, all containing \hand{$\Delta$}$_1$ which is indeed a strict homotopy interval\hand{,}
as it represent\over{\unsure{g}}{s}s the \xout{functor} presheaf on (Cat)
\[ C \longmapsto \text{ set of all cribles in } C \ . \]
If we take the choice I = two-point category \over{\illegible{1}}{e}quivalent to the final one\hand{,}
this also is a strict homotopy interval, as it represents the functor
\[ C \longmapsto \text{ set of all full subcategories of } C \ , \]
hence again an intersection law. The first \inserted[typed]{homotopy} interval though is a lot more
important than the second, because the first one is ``characteristic''
namely sufficient for checking the homotopy relation between any two
maps in (Cat), whereas the second is'nt. A ``\uline{perfect}'' homotopy interval
would be one which is both \uline{strict} (hence contractible) and \uline{characteristic}.

